Package: epicurves
Title: Likelihood Inference for Epidemic Curves with Stochastic Compartmental Models and Growth-Curve Ensembles
Version: 0.0.0.9000
Authors@R: c(
    person("Raydonal", "Ospina", , "raydonal@castlab.org", role = c("aut", "cre")),
    person("Jaciele", "Oliveira", , "jaciele.jesus@ufpe.br", role = "aut"),
    person("Cristiano", "Ferraz", , "cferraz@castlab.org", role = "aut"),
    person("André", "Leite", , "leite@castlab.org", role = "aut")
    )
Description: Likelihood-based tools for epidemic curves. Stochastic
    compartmental models are fitted to surveillance counts through the
    Gaussian (linear noise) approximation of the underlying density-dependent
    Markov jump process and a Kalman filter: any mass-action model can be
    declared by its events, observed through the incidence of events (new
    cases, new deaths) or the prevalence of compartments, with under-reporting
    and time-varying rates. Logistic, Gompertz and Richards growth curves are
    fitted to daily counts by Poisson or negative binomial likelihood,
    combined with Akaike weights, and the series is divided into phases by
    penalised likelihood or by anytime-valid sequential e-detectors. The
    package accompanies two articles derived from Oliveira's doctoral thesis
    and reproduces their analyses.
